WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.931
A multi-step equation still asks where two quantities agree

00:00:03.931 --> 00:00:06.678
These two lines meet at one input

00:00:06.678 --> 00:00:14.133
Rather than guessing from the picture, we will transform the equality through steps that preserve exactly the same solution.

00:00:15.433 --> 00:00:19.992
First distribute the factor two to every term in its parentheses

00:00:19.992 --> 00:00:25.794
Two copies of x contribute two x and two copies of three contribute six

00:00:25.794 --> 00:00:30.767
This rewrite changes only the notation, so the graphed relationship remains unchanged.

00:00:32.067 --> 00:00:34.513
Now subtract x from both sides

00:00:34.513 --> 00:00:38.997
One x remains on the left, while the right becomes eleven

00:00:38.997 --> 00:00:47.967
Both graphs have changed, but their equality still occurs at the same horizontal position because the identical quantity was removed from each.

00:00:49.267 --> 00:00:56.559
Subtract six from both sides to isolate the input five, then check it in the original equation

00:00:56.559 --> 00:00:58.704
Both original sides become sixteen

00:00:58.704 --> 00:01:05.567
A term does not change sign by traveling; subtraction from both sides explains that familiar shortcut.
